          body: `Young children learn words best from live, responsive people. The American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) recommends avoiding screen media other than video chatting for children under 18 months. For 18 to 24 months, if parents want to introduce digital media, it should be high-quality content watched together. For ages 2 to 5, the AAP suggests no more than 1 hour a day of high-quality programming.\n\nThe World Health Organization (WHO) guidelines for under-fives say sedentary screen time is not recommended for babies and 1-year-olds, and for 2-year-olds it should be no more than 1 hour a day, with less being better. When children are sitting still, WHO encourages reading and storytelling with a caregiver instead.\n\nVideo calls with grandparents and other family are a reasonable exception, especially when an adult sits with the child and keeps the conversation going.`,
