          title: `Hearing checks: newborn screening and beyond`,
          body: `Hearing is the foundation of spoken language, which is why many countries offer newborn hearing screening. In England, the NHS Newborn Hearing Screening Programme offers every baby a quick test, usually before 4 weeks of age (it can be done up to 3 months). The first test uses a small earpiece that plays soft clicks; if the result is not clear, a second test may be done. An unclear result is often caused by something temporary, such as fluid in the ear or an unsettled baby, rather than hearing loss.\n\nA clear newborn screen does not rule out hearing problems later, for example after ear infections. Whatever your baby's age, if you are worried about their hearing, or speech is slow to develop, ask for a hearing test. It is usually one of the first checks when a child is late to talk.`,
